Z||000008||Seanh||06-17-2001||03:56 PM||||I will check my manual for this, however just assuming by the name, its involves somthing with the rack perhaps? I don't have the new rack on yet. I am not going to put it on until this problem is taked care of. I put a pair of pliers on the coupling where the shaft connects to pinion, and the steering wheel still had about the same amount of play in it as it did before I removed the rack, and after the accident.<P>BTW, for those of you who don't know about it yet, this car was ran into a 7" railroad tie on the edge of a dirt track and the passenger side tire took the impact, not the body. I'm pretty sure somthing is a little more screwed up then the rack preload. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||06-17-2001||05:08 PM||||There are several places the slop could be. You won't know until the column and lower shaft are out for inspection. Free play in the column and shaft should be very near zero.||||reg||1
